• This is not an agent.
• Just one LLM call with a tool bound.
• Meant for simple math queries.
When the orchestrator routes to "math", it uses this chain:
answer = tool_chain.invoke({"input": user_input})
⸻
🤖 2) Agent (multi-step, tool-using brain)
def build_agent():
   prompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_messages([...])
   agent_core = create_openai_functions_agent(
       llm=llm,
       tools=TOOLS,
       prompt=prompt
   )
   agent_executor = AgentExecutor(
       agent=agent_core,
       tools=TOOLS,
       verbose=True
   )
   return agent_executor
• Here, agent_executor owns the loop:
• think → tool → think → tool → final answer
• It can call calculator and risk_score multiple times.
The orchestrator uses it in two ways:
• directly (for general queries):
result = agent_executor.invoke({"input": user_input})
answer = result["output"]
• inside the workflow (see next).
⸻
🛤 3) Workflow / Pipeline (fixed sequence, developer-controlled)
def build_underwriting_workflow(agent_executor: AgentExecutor):
   clean_step = RunnableLambda(clean_text)
   agent_step = RunnableLambda(run_agent_on_text)
   format_step = format_prompt | llm | StrOutputParser()
   workflow = clean_step | agent_step | format_step
   return workflow
This workflow is deterministic:
1. clean_step → cleans user text
2. agent_step → always calls the agent in a fixed way
3. format_step → always formats result
Order cannot change.
LLM cannot decide to skip or reorder steps.
Inside agent_step, the agent still has its own internal dynamic tool logic — but the outer pipeline order is fixed.
The orchestrator uses this workflow when route == "underwriting":
answer = workflow.invoke(user_input)
