Excellent catch — and this shows your understanding is becoming deep.
You noticed this line:
“Here the LLM gets access to the calculator tool and typically uses it ONCE…”
And you asked:
Why am I saying tool calling usually uses the tool only once?
Why can’t tool calling handle multiple tool calls just like agents?
This is THE RIGHT QUESTION, and the answer will make everything clear.
Let’s go step-by-step and end this confusion permanently.
⸻
🟦 1) ✔ TOOL CALLING CAN technically make multiple calls
Yes — you are right.
Tool calling is NOT LIMITED to one call.
Modern LLMs (GPT-4o, GPT-5, Claude 3, Gemini 1.5)
CAN:
	•	call a tool
	•	use that output
	•	call the tool AGAIN
	•	and again
	•	then return a final answer
So the limitation is NOT in the model.
The limitation is in how the chain is built.
Let’s break this down.
⸻
🟩 2) Why pure tool calling behaves like “one shot” in LangChain
When you write:
prompt | llm.bind_tools([...]) | parser
You are building a ONE-SHOT CHAIN:
Input → LLM → (maybe tool) → Output → END
Once the model outputs a tool call like:
{"tool": "calculator", "arguments": {…}}
LangChain does:
	•	run the tool
	•	return the tool result
	•	STOP THE CHAIN
There is no second pass of the model unless YOU manually feed it again.
🔥 So by default:
The pipeline ends after ONE tool call.
Even though the LLM wants to call more tools, the pipeline structure stops it.
This is by design — to keep pipelines predictable.
⸻
🟧 3) Agents solve this limitation
Agents in LangChain create a loop around tool calling:
THINK → TOOL → THINK → TOOL → THINK → FINAL ANSWER
Agents automatically:
	•	take tool result
	•	feed it back into model
	•	interpret the next thought
	•	run next tool
	•	repeat until final answer
This loop does NOT exist in simple tool calling.
That loop is the ONLY difference.
Tool calling has no loop → 1 call
Agent has loop → many calls
⸻
🔥 Let’s make it CRYSTAL CLEAR with a simple visual
🟦 Tool calling (default)
User
  ↓
LLM decides a tool call
  ↓
Tool runs
  ↓
Return result
  ⛔ STOP
🟩 Agent
User
  ↓
LLM: Thought #1
  ↓
Tool call #1
  ↓
LLM: Thought #2
  ↓
Tool call #2
  ↓
LLM: Thought #3
  ↓
...
  ↓
LLM: Final answer
Agents AUTOMATICALLY loop.
Tool-calling chains DO NOT.
Unless you manually create the loop (which is what LangGraph eventually does).
⸻
🟦 4) Why doesn’t basic tool calling auto-loop?
Because simple tool-calling chains were designed to be:
	•	predictable
	•	non-recursive
	•	safe
	•	single-step for API integration
	•	transparent
	•	cheap
If every simple chain auto-looped:
	•	infinite loops could occur
	•	unpredictable behavior
	•	costly multi-step runs
	•	developer loses control
	•	output becomes unstable
So LangChain intentionally made basic tool calling one-shot.
Agents were created to solve the “multi-step” requirement safely.
⸻
🟩 5) Does the LLM WANT to call multiple tools sometimes?
YES.
LLMs like GPT-4o can do multi-step tool reasoning.
But in basic tool-calling pipelines,
there is no mechanism to feed the tool result back to the model.
So the LLM does:
	•	first tool call
	•	returns
	•	chain ends
Agents introduce a reasoning loop.
⸻
🟦 6) A simple example that proves the difference
User:
First add 10 + 20, then multiply the result by 3.
Basic Tool Calling (no loop):
	•	LLM creates ONE tool call:
"add(10,20)"
	•	Tool returns 30
	•	Chain stops
	•	LLM never gets to “multiply by 3”
Agent:
	•	Thought: I should add 10+20 → tool call
	•	Result = 30
	•	Thought: Now multiply 30 × 3 → tool call
	•	Result = 90
	•	Final answer.
See the difference?
⸻
🟧 7) So in summary:
✔ Tool Calling (basic chain):
	•	LLM can call a tool
	•	Chain stops after the first tool execution
	•	No reasoning loop
	•	No multi-step planning
	•	Only continues if you manually chain multiple calls
✔ Agent:
	•	Adds a reasoning loop
	•	Automatically chains tool → thought → tool → thought
	•	Supports multi-step plans
	•	Supports error handling, retries, backtracking
	•	More powerful but less predictable
⸻
🟩 8) WHY LangChain separates them?
Because they solve different use cases:
✔ Tool calling:
	•	Simple tasks
	•	One-shot utility
	•	API integration
	•	No need for complex reasoning
✔ Agents:
	•	Complex tasks
	•	Multi-step workflows
	•	Tools depending on previous tools
	•	Real assistants
	•	Dynamic task solving
⸻
⭐ Final Crystal-Clear Answer to Your Question
🚨 If tool calling “only uses one tool”, it’s NOT because:
❌ the model can’t
❌ the tool can’t
❌ the LLM is weak
✔ It’s because:
basic tool-calling pipelines do NOT have a loop to continue reasoning.
✔ Agents ADD this loop.
✔ Workflows PROVIDE fixed sequence defined by YOU.
